## Printer side

- [X] Add StatusClaimed to the Go model (exists in the DB constraint)
- [X] Printer store/service
- [X] Atomic claim endpoint: `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, set printer_id, claimed_at, attempts+1
- [X] Status reporting endpoints: printing -> printed, or failed with last_error
- [X] Heartbeat endpoint updating printers.last_seen_at / last_job_at
- [X] Recover stuck claims: back to pending after a timeout, give up after max attempts
- [X] Expose printer health: computed `online` field (last_heartbeat_at within N seconds) in the printer response
- [X] Treat a claim as a heartbeat
- [ ] ... also during quiet hours: `ClaimOldest` returns `ErrInQuietHours` before the query that sets `last_heartbeat_at`, so a printer that only polls claims shows offline all night
- [ ] Reset `processed_at` (and `printed_at`) when a message is claimed again after a retry; today it keeps the previous attempt's `processed_at`, earlier than the new `claimed_at`
- [ ] Decide whether a printer may hold several claims at once (today it can claim again before finishing the previous message)
- [ ] `DELETE /admin/printers/{id}/quiet_hours` also sets `timezone` to NULL, while create always stores the default timezone

## Security hardening

- [X] Strip control characters (except newline) from content to prevent ESC/POS command injection ...
- [ ] ... and filter again on the Pi
- [X] Strip control characters from printer error strings (`failed`, `heartbeat`); a NUL byte made Postgres reject the update (500)
- [ ] Limit the length of printer error strings (`last_error`, `printers.error`); only the 16 KB body limit applies today
- [ ] Rate limiting per user and per IP
- [ ] Only use middleware.RealIP behind a trusted reverse proxy (X-Forwarded-For is spoofable)
- [X] Public GET /messages/{id} leaks sender_ip to anyone with the UUID: use a slimmer public response type (route since replaced by the authenticated GET /user/messages/{id})

